---
name: Appwrite Edge
description: The router and orchestrator that run Appwrite Sites and Functions. Every request maps to a deployment, and every deployment runs on open-runtimes/orchestrator.
github: https://github.com/open-runtimes/orchestrator
---

Appwrite Edge runs customer code in six regions. It has two halves: a router that decides where each request goes, and an orchestrator that runs the code.

## The router

Appwrite Sites needed something in front of every customer domain. I built that router for the Sites launch in 2025. It now serves about 11 million requests a day.

Each request resolves a domain to a deployment and a region, then to a running runtime. Preview deployments, redirect rules, custom error pages and cache invalidation all live in the router. When a deployment changes, the region publishes an event and the edge drops its cached rule and runtime. Runtimes scale per project, with warm capacity kept on spare nodes.

## open-runtimes/orchestrator

The orchestrator is the next generation orchestrator for Appwrite Functions and builds. It replaces an executor that drove Docker on a single host. I wrote the design docs and nearly all of the code in 2026, and it runs Appwrite Cloud's builds in production.

It runs four kinds of workload behind one API. Deployments serve traffic, pools keep warm pods ready, sandboxes run untrusted code alone, and jobs run builds. The same API works on Docker for local development and on Kubernetes in production.

Build artifacts are mounted as filesystem images rather than extracted, so large deployments start fast. A node mount agent does the mount on the host, so no pod running customer code needs a privileged container next to it.
